questions.explanation_english:
All three conditions—Dwarfism, Sickle Cell Disease, and Acid Attack Victims—were introduced for the first time under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 and were absent in the 1995 Act. Under the 1995 legislation, only 7 categories were recognized. In the 2016 Act's Schedule:
- Dwarfism is categorized under Locomotor Disability (defined as a medical or genetic condition resulting in an adult height of 4 feet 10 inches or less);
- Acid Attack Victims are recognized under Locomotor Disability, acknowledging psychological and physical disfigurement;
- Sickle Cell Disease is recognized under Blood Disorders.
Including these conditions ensures that affected students and citizens gain statutory protection, affirmative action, and educational accommodations.
